#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153090} :activity/id #uuid "21c31b1b-d40c-5fbe-b15a-999554ec43b1" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152179 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63580eb-b476-51fa-a005-560f6e57d911"} :step/id #uuid "718f6754-40c5-5b42-a425-d91992e573a7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153096}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15e8730b-c59a-5897-8c83-073b54f517a9"} :step/id #uuid "c503a970-4f74-565b-a10f-e1aa26b3aade" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152181 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c8f0fa1-a86a-577f-bdc9-fc7427faa18d"} :step/id #uuid "dac3b326-03e4-531d-be2f-af0a55237db9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153365 :tutorial/content "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven a point $A(1,1),$ let $A'$ be the image of $A$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ How can we find the coordinates of $A'?$\n\nFirst, we create a column vector $\\mathbf{x}$ containing the coordinates of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $T\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $T \\mathbf{x},$ contains the coordinates of $A'.$ Therefore, $A'(-1,-2).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4d5c8f46-d41f-54e7-b904-4a0dd5c53af2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4090 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "47319bd6-6a77-5e3c-a0b8-c11cb72d7ee1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153366 :tutorial/content "Consider a non-singular linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ and a line that is given by the vector equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{d}t, \\qquad\n\tt \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nNote that, for the above equation to represent a line (and not just a point), we must have $\\mathbf{d} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nNow, using the linearity of the transformation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(r) & =T(p+dt) \\\\  & =T(p)+T(td) \\\\  & =T(p)+tT(d) \\\\  & ={p}^{'}+t{d}^{'},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{p})=\\mathbf{p}'$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{d})=\\mathbf{d}'.$\n\nBecause $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular and $\\mathbf{d} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ we know that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{d}) \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ which means $\\mathbf{d}' \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\mathbf{p}'+t\\mathbf{d}'$ represents a straight line, and we can make the following conclusion:\n\n> *A non-singular linear transformation maps a straight line onto a straight line.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "e0b93f1f-ffda-51cc-87ea-a5906a94f7d9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4091 :tutorial/title "The Proof That a Linear Transformation Maps a Straight Line Onto a Straight Line"} :step/id #uuid "c1f70e8e-5b79-56df-8c86-d7683913fb7f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153373 :tutorial/content "Linear transformations of a plane have the following important property: \n\n> *A non-singular linear transformation in the plane maps a straight line into a straight line. *\n\nTherefore, to find the image of a line $l$ under the action of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ (represented by its standard matrix $T$), we do the following:\n\n- Find two distinct points $A$ and $B$ that lie on the line $l.$\n\n- Compute the images $A'$ and $B'$ of the points $A$ and $B$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\n- Write down the equation of the line that passes through $A'$ and $B'.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9501e857-f231-5e41-a526-334a1fed20e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4119 :tutorial/title "The Image of a Line Under the Action of a Linear Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "451dbef8-606a-5a73-83ab-109b7eb5a1d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153089}}] :activity/title "Linear Transformations of Points and Lines in the Plane" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152349}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049063 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152179 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151974 :question/id #uuid "d2dafa69-6a9b-5f99-a16d-5fe5529ffd66" :question/math-academy-id "e-6704" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n![](images/11/11b0a0434116475ce544b737e282fd34f252aa781a72e7bdedf1d868d47087c0.png)\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $B',$ the image of $B(2,-3)$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $B$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a column vector $\\mathbf{x}$ containing the coordinates of $B{:}$\n\n![](images/de/de0e0e242c55d338db39c80ce9b34c4a841aacef15a2368e1c00610ece9d2679.png)\n\nNow, we compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $T\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $T \\mathbf{x},$ contains the coordinates of $B'.$ Therefore, $B'(3,-5).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63580eb-b476-51fa-a005-560f6e57d911" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d7242e-e655-57f3-9f0c-134be9b01c96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342320} {:answer/id #uuid "262de546-7e96-56a3-9dc6-d91fca267a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342562} {:answer/id #uuid "457fc1aa-44c3-5380-bb9f-44dd3c8421d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342810} {:answer/id #uuid "85d16cf8-1929-5dca-9938-79dcf848d827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343387} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf698f4-5ba3-5a99-bc10-8892ad7b9148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342562} :answer-field/id #uuid "abcb4a57-9e42-537d-8bd8-ae3f5f9fcc1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344795}] :question/id #uuid "dd69ce92-0c38-503f-b13d-fc02108de3ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-77543" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point ${A}^{′}$, the image of $A(-1,1)$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $A$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $A:$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${A}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${A}^{′}(2,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85ea63c1-961d-5e8b-