onclusion, we get  $a=-6,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b9fc411-d727-58c3-b130-7cb8806396aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455067} {:answer/id #uuid "6651a84a-abb1-53a5-8762-4f56861de479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455088} {:answer/id #uuid "85b5f122-b544-5270-a1c3-306a799a62e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455143} {:answer/id #uuid "b787992e-f51f-5213-a591-68b1b37d8b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455225} {:answer/id #uuid "c81b241b-7949-5f59-a9cc-99f9b9e79c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455067} :answer-field/id #uuid "86414dc6-5142-5c5b-92b4-cd993eb839dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455379}] :question/id #uuid "dde0c5ba-d69f-5523-84e3-29d85ec2262a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80391" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $E=\\begin{bmatrix}-32 & 40 \\\\ -30 & 38\\end{bmatrix}.$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8$ are the eigenvalues of $E$, and\n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=E,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=8.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1968 :topic/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060681} :activity/id #uuid "84d7f054-fed4-524c-b241-f09c7fd42e5e" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060940 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can apply some operations to the augmented matrix that do not change the set of solutions for the corresponding system. These operations are called **elementary row operations**. \n\nAfter applying an **elementary row operation** to the augmented matrix, the new corresponding system will have the same solutions.\n\nThere are three types of elementary row operations:\n\n- switching any two rows: \n\n$$\nR_i \\leftrightarrow R_j\n$$\n\n- multiplying (each element of) a row by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nR_i := k R_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- adding a multiple of one row to another row: \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j+k R_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\nIn the notations above, the \"$:=$\" symbol is called the **assignment operator.** We use this to assign new values to every element of a given row.\n\nFor example, the row operation $R_1 := 2 R_1$ means, \"create a new row $1$ by taking the old row $1$ and multiplying it by $2.$\" When we do this, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-1) & 2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA few final points on notation.\n\n- We use the similarity symbol ($\\sim$) to indicate when one matrix is transformed into another matrix using elementary row operations.\n\n- The assignment operator $:=$ is sometimes called the \"walrus operator\" because it looks like a walrus on its side!" :tutorial/id #uuid "7aa753a7-1450-53dd-8e61-71e07952cecb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3150 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3df97c2e-febb-5f72-be71-e1d739dbbd92" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060682}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059771 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1afe82-86ba-5a15-9037-74717692ecb2"} :step/id #uuid "46eeafc2-7d22-58a7-bc12-127e3c86f554" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c27a3f3c-77c7-55e9-8714-1f24c4a48063"} :step/id #uuid "797229e4-e202-5744-a0d3-6e8c4cae4059" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "691b9107-a935-5965-b3ac-0f3a193ce9bd"} :step/id #uuid "cfe1d425-2185-5011-8249-58d7c6bd7ec8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059774 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0464b51e-8568-521e-96a5-a65c04569c2a"} :step/id #uuid "0daa33bf-3f45-5650-9a9c-808f765e3f26"}] :activity/title "Elementary Row Operations" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059883}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059580 :question/id #uuid "836f2f1b-5dd4-5dba-81cc-eb9ffe7db8d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4693" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2 := R_2 +(- 4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1$ means that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4+(-4)(1) & 2+(-4)(2) & -1+(-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1afe82-86ba-5a15-9037-74717692ecb2" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525196 :question-generator/id #uuid "0effb7b2-a78d-5aa5-8039-e8746375ef79" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186059771.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5a9bba7-f581-5372-a873-0fc17a55fb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/valu